{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And they went out into the wilderness of Shur.</b> It is called the wilderness of Shur (שור) on account of the contemplation of wisdom, for thus Israel are called by the name (Deuteronomy 32:15) ישורון (Jeshurun), and this is from the language of (Numbers 23:9) אשורנו ('I see him'). And so Israel were going from ascent to ascent, מחיל אל חיל — from strength to strength: from the Sea of Reeds, where they attained the glory of the Shekhinah, to the wilderness of Shur, where they were gazing and entering the pardes at what they had seen upon the sea. And the wilderness of Shur is the wilderness of Etham, as it is written in parashat Masei: ויסעו מפני החירות ויעברו בתוך הים המדברה וילכו דרך שלשת ימים במדבר איתם ויחנו במרה." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'And they went out into the wilderness of Shur' — a place where Israel desired to make themselves into banners, row by row (שורות שורות). Another interpretation: מדבר שור — until the Torah was given, the world was a מדבר (wilderness); once they received the Torah, it became עולם שור — a world of שור, that is, a wall (חומה). And so in the time to come, Zion, which is a wilderness, as it says (Isaiah 64:9) ״ציון מדבר היתה״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, will make it שור, as it says (Zechariah 2:9) ״ואני אהיה לה נאם ה׳ חומת אש סביב״." "<b>And they went three days in the wilderness.</b> I have already mentioned above that every act of the wilderness was a pure trial, and their journey through the great and awesome wilderness with their women and children, and their standing without water for three days in summertime — there is no trial like this in the world. And when they came to Marah, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that the waters were sweet and turned bitter, and all of this was for a trial, in the manner of what it says: ושם נסהו. And when Moses prayed over this and cast a bitter thing into the waters and they returned to their sweetness, all of this was to strengthen the trial and to implant in their comprehending souls the trait of בטחון (trust). And therefore He wished to sweeten them with a bitter thing — a miracle within a miracle. Hence our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: ויורהו ה׳ עץ — Rabbi Nathan says: it was the willow (הרדיפוני); Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i says: it was an olive tree; and some say: it was the roots of a fig tree. In any case, whether bitter or bitter, it was bitter. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: how wondrous are His deeds and His ways beyond the ways of flesh and blood, for thus it is written regarding Elisha (II Kings 2:20): ״קחו לי צלוחית חדשה ושימו שם מלח״ — He placed a thing that spoils into that which is spoiled, to perform a miracle within a miracle." "<b>And Rabbi Hananel wrote</b> that what it says, 'And they went three days in the wilderness,' the distance was a three-day journey, and they traversed it in a single day, and the lack of water was for one day. And he explained the reason, that it is not in the nature of a person (בן עולם) to survive three days without water, let alone the children and the women who were pregnant and nursing among them." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'And they went three days in the wilderness and found no water' — the waters they had drawn for themselves amid the cloven walls of the Sea of Reeds were finished from them at that hour. The darshanim of remez said: מים ('water') means nothing but Torah, as it says (Isaiah 55:1) ״הוי כל צמא לכו למים״. From here they enacted that the Torah scroll should be read three days a week, so that they would not go three days without Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:57.124859560Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}